<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Table 1</span>: </span>Aggregation results of MV, SP and ISP.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that MV, SP and ISP all select the candidate with maximum advantage, that is, they respectively maximize <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Adv_{MV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Adv_{MV}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Adv_{SP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Adv_{SP}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Adv_{ISP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Adv_{ISP}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since for all three, the sum of advantages over all labels is zero by probability normalization, effective aggregation requires the correct label <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to attain the largest advantage.
This observation provides the foundation for our subsequent theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Model ‣ Beyond Majority Voting: LLM Aggregation by Leveraging Higher-Order Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, ISP in <a href="#alg2" title="In 4.2 From Surprisingly Popular to Inverse Surprisingly Popular ‣ 4 Leveraging Second-order Information ‣ Beyond Majority Voting: LLM Aggregation by Leveraging Higher-Order Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> outperforms MV, which in turn outperforms SP, in expectation. That is, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Adv_{ISP}(s^{*})]\geq\mathbb{E}[Adv_{MV}(s^{*})]\geq\mathbb{E}[Adv_{SP}(s^{*})]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Adv_{ISP}(s^{*})]\geq\mathbb{E}[Adv_{MV}(s^{*})]\geq\mathbb{E}[Adv_{SP}(s^{*})].</annotation></semantics></math> More specifically, it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Adv_{ISP}(s^{*})-Adv_{MV}(s^{*})]=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j\in[N]\setminus\{i\}}(Kx_{i}-1)(Kx_{j}-1)^{2}}{(N-1)K(K-1)^{3}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Adv_{ISP}(s^{*})-Adv_{MV}(s^{*})]=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j\in[N]\setminus\{i\}}(Kx_{i}-1)(Kx_{j}-1)^{2}}{(N-1)K(K-1)^{3}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Adv_{MV}(s^{*})-Adv_{SP}(s^{*})]=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j\in[N]\setminus\{i\}}(Kx_{i}-1)(Kx_{j}-1)^{2}}{(N-1)K(K-1)^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Adv_{MV}(s^{*})-Adv_{SP}(s^{*})]=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j\in[N]\setminus\{i\}}(Kx_{i}-1)(Kx_{j}-1)^{2}}{(N-1)K(K-1)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4.2 From Surprisingly Popular to Inverse Surprisingly Popular ‣ 4 Leveraging Second-order Information ‣ Beyond Majority Voting: LLM Aggregation by Leveraging Higher-Order Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, as the number of options increases, ISP’s superiority over MV gradually diminishes, as <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Adv_{ISP}(s^{*})-Adv_{MV}(s^{*})]\simeq\Theta(1/K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Adv_{ISP}(s^{*})-Adv_{MV}(s^{*})]\simeq\Theta(1/K)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, MV remains consistently better than SP, say <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Adv_{MV}(s^{*})-Adv_{SP}(s^{*})\simeq\Theta(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Adv_{MV}(s^{*})-Adv_{SP}(s^{*})\simeq\Theta(1)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. This effect arises from the exploration component in ISP, whose efficiency decreases as <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> grows. In other words, when agents make incorrect predictions, exploring the correct label becomes harder. Hence, in practical settings where there are only a few options, ISP provides stronger guarantees and is more desirable to use.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Practical Estimation of Second-order Information</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now return to the practical setting, where only finitely many samples are available, say a dataset consisting of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> questions and corresponding responses from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> LLM agents, to estimate the second-order information <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(A_{i}|A_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(A_{i}|A_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can naturally employ empirical conditional probabilities to estimate, that is,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbb{P}}(A_{i}=s_{k}|A_{j}=s_{l})=\frac{\#\{A_{i}=s_{k},A_{j}=s_{l}\}}{\#\{A_{j}=s_{l}\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbb{P}}(A_{i}=s_{k}|A_{j}=s_{l})=\frac{\#\{A_{i}=s_{k},A_{j}=s_{l}\}}{\#\{A_{j}=s_{l}\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Although <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},...,A_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},...,A_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> are not independent, we still have the following theorem after random shuffling, showing that when data size <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large, the empirical advantage of ISP remains greater than that of MV.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Model ‣ Beyond Majority Voting: LLM Aggregation by Leveraging Higher-Order Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, when there are <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. questions, it holds that for every question, with probability at least <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\widehat{Adv}_{ISP}(s^{*})-Adv_{MV}(s^{*})]\gtrsim\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j\in[N]\setminus\{i\}}(Kx_{i}-1)(Kx_{j}-1)^{2}}{(N-1)K(K-1)^{3}}-\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{\frac{1}{{M}}\log(\frac{1}{\delta})})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\widehat{Adv}_{ISP}(s^{*})-Adv_{MV}(s^{*})]\gtrsim\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j\in[N]\setminus\{i\}}(Kx_{i}-1)(Kx_{j}-1)^{2}}{(N-1)K(K-1)^{3}}-\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{\frac{1}{{M}}\log(\frac{1}{\delta})}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> suppresses some logarithmic factors in <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, the expectation is only taken over predictions <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},...,A_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},...,A_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> to this question and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{Adv}_{ISP}(s^{*})=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ind\{a_{i}=s^{*}\}-\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{N-1}\sum_{j\in[N]\setminus\{i\}}\frac{1}{K-1}\sum_{a\in S\setminus\{a_{j}\}}\widehat{\mathbb{P}}(A_{i}=s^{*}|A_{j}=a)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mi>𝟙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Adv}_{ISP}(s^{*})=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ind\{a_{i}=s^{*}\}-\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{N-1}\sum_{j\in[N]\setminus\{i\}}\frac{1}{K-1}\sum_{a\in S\setminus\{a_{j}\}}\widehat{\mathbb{P}}(A_{i}=s^{*}|A_{j}=a).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We organize our experiments as follows. In <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Evaluation on Simulated Data ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Beyond Majority Voting: LLM Aggregation by Leveraging Higher-Order Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, we use simulations to demonstrate the advantages of ISP over MV and SP. <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Adapted Optimal Weight Algorithm Without Ground Truth ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Beyond Majority Voting: LLM Aggregation by Leveraging Higher-Order Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> introduces our methodology, showing how second-order information inspires the estimation of optimal weights in OW for unavailable accuracies. In <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Real-world Datasets ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Beyond Majority Voting: LLM Aggregation by Leveraging Higher-Order Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Evaluation Results on Real-World Datasets ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Beyond Majority Voting: LLM Aggregation by Leveraging Higher-Order Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, we apply our methods to reinforcement learning with human feedback (RLHF) and healthcare datasets, illustrating how both OW and ISP can improve upon MV in practice.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Evaluation on Simulated Data</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We simulate a setting with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=4</annotation></semantics></math> agents whose accuracies are fixed at 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, and 0.9, respectively, to mimic LLM performance heterogeneity. We randomly generate <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=10,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=10,000</annotation></semantics></math> questions and vary the number of answer choices <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\{2,4,6,8,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\{2,4,6,8,10\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For MV, ties are broken uniformly at random. For SP and ISP, we leverage empirical second-order information to compute the advantage; for example, in <a href="#alg2" title="In 4.2 From Surprisingly Popular to Inverse Surprisingly Popular ‣ 4 Leveraging Second-order Information ‣ Beyond Majority Voting: LLM Aggregation by Leveraging Higher-Order Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we input <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbb{P}}(A_{i}=s_{k}|A_{j}=s_{l})=\frac{\#\{A_{i}=s_{k},A_{j}=s_{l}\}}{\#\{A_{j}=s_{l}\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbb{P}}(A_{i}=s_{k}|A_{j}=s_{l})=\frac{\#\{A_{i}=s_{k},A_{j}=s_{l}\}}{\#\{A_{j}=s_{l}\}}</annotation></semantics></math> within the dataset. We present our results in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In 5.1 Evaluation on Simulated Data ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Beyond Majority Voting: LLM Aggregation by Leveraging Higher-Order Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We use OPT to denote the Bayesian optimal algorithm OW with clairvoyant access to the best weights, and Single Best to represent the strongest agent.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Experimental Results: We report the overall accuracy for different values of <math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. In the tables, the best results are highlighted in <span id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>, and the second-best results are <span id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>.
</figcaption>
